\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs32\b newVM CUDA (cuFFT) vs OpenCL (clFFT) Performance Benchmark\b0\fs22\par
\fs24\b Complex-to-complex FFT/IFFT, single precision, N = 4096 .. 16384\b0\par
\fs24\b Investigating whether a CUDA-backed capability is worth adding to newVMCL.pas\b0\par
\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Machine:\b0  Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14650HX (hybrid: 8 P-cores/16 threads + 8 E-cores/8 threads = 24 logical CPUs), 31 GiB RAM\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b OS:\b0  Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, kernel 7.0.0-30-generic\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b GPU:\b0  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU, 8151 MiB VRAM, driver 595.84, compute capability 12.0 (Blackwell) - same device perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf and perf/newVMCLfft8192performance.rtf already benchmarked\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Libraries:\b0  CUDA 13.1 (nvcc V13.1.115), cuFFT/cuFFTW from cuda-13-1, OpenCL/clFFT via the same libOpenCL.so.1 / libclFFT.so.2 newVMCL.pas already links against, FFTW3 (libfftw3f) - same library newVMComplexSingle.pas uses on this machine\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Build:\b0  nvcc -O2 -arch=native newVMCUDAvsCLFFTbench.cu -o newVMCUDAvsCLFFTbench -I/usr/local/include -L/usr/local/lib64 -lcufft -lOpenCL -lclFFT -lfftw3f\par
\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Motivation:\b0  This is a standalone C++/CUDA program (newVMCUDAvsCLFFTbench.cu, committed at the repo root alongside newVMbench.lpr/newVMCLbench.lpr/newVMCLfft8192bench.lpr - same "answer the question with a throwaway harness first" spirit as that last one), NOT wired into newVMtest.lpi/newVMCL.pas - purely investigative. It exercises cuFFT, clFFT and FFTW under IDENTICAL methodology in the SAME process/run, at N = 4096, 6144, 8192, 12288 and 16384 (spanning the requested 4096-16k range; 6144/12288 added between the round numbers since neither library requires a power-of-two length).\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Methodology:\b0  For each library, two numbers per size: (1) "replan/call" - a naive create-plan + execute + destroy-plan loop, 100 iterations, exactly matching how newVMCL.pas's FFT()/IFFT() are implemented TODAY (a fresh clFFT plan on every call, per that unit's own code) - the direct apples-to-apples number against the CURRENT clFFT integration; (2) "plan reused" - a plan created ONCE, then executed (forward and inverse) 100 times against that same persistent plan - the best case IF a future implementation cached plans. Host FFTW is measured the same way, with the same FFTW_ESTIMATE|FFTW_PRESERVE_INPUT flags newVMComplexSingle.pas's own FFT()/IFFT() already use - FFTW's planner is cheap enough that even its "replan/call" number is a fair proxy for ordinary CPU usage. All timings are wall-clock around the operation with the GPU synchronized before stopping the clock (clFinish / cudaDeviceSynchronize), so they include real kernel-launch+execute latency, not just enqueue time.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Correctness:\b0  Round-trip (forward then inverse, compared back against the original random input) at every size, for all three libraries - all confirmed numerically correct (residuals ~1e-5, same order as each other) once a bug in this benchmark's OWN residual check was fixed (see Notes below); this is not a claim about newVMCL.pas's own test coverage, which already verifies FFT/IFFT correctness at N=16 via newVMTests.pas's TVMobjCLTests.\par
\fs22\par
